  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 121 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nTuesday and Wednesday continue to trend as our most active days of\nthis coming week as models see the closed upper-level low push\neastward across Southern Nevada while the upper-level high settles\nover the Gulf States. This positioning will lead to decent amount\nof moisture funneling from both gulfs into the Southern Great\nPlains for those days. Added to this will also be the potential\nfor remnants of Hurricane Simon and other tropical systems to be\ningested into this flow, which would result in an amplification of\nthe expected moisture. If the latest mid to long range models are\nto be believed then PWAT values across the Eastern Panhandles can\nrange from 1.5 inches all the way to 2 inches in our far\nSoutheast. Such values are very well above normal for this time of\nyear and can easily lead to another round of heavy to torrential\nrainfall, especially across our east. Added to this has been a\ncontinued trend of increase instability and wind shear that would\nsuggest strong to severe thunderstorms being a possibility for\nTuesday evening going into early Wednesday. However, there are\nsome caveats that can see this active pattern not reach its full\npotential. The first of which being the position of the upper-\nlevel low as it move over the Panhandles. Presently most models\nhave said low taking a more northerly track, which typically opens\nthe Panhandles up to a bit more to the subsidence and dry air\nsections of the low. While this has done little to diminish our\nchances across our east in most model runs, it has seen the\nNorthwestern Panhandles a too stable with chances over there only\nrunning around 10 to 20 percent. These lower chances can spread\neven further across the Panhandles should the low decide to drop\nslightly more south and place itself directly on top of us.\nSecondary to this issue will be the timing of the associated cold\nfront following said low. As it stand present model agreement has\nthe main cold front arriving sometime late Wednesday into\nThursday. However, a few runs, have picked up on the potential of\na much earlier arrival of the front, which is more typical for our\narea this time of year. An earlier arrival of the front would\ndampen our rainfall amounts as the atmosphere would be a lot\nstabler, leading to a more stratiformic rain event at its best or\nno rain at all at its worst. Either way present NBM is favoring\nfor some form of precipitation continuing with 20 to 40 percent\nchances holding through Friday morning. Otherwise, look for much\ncooler temperatures to follow this activity with high in the mid\nto upper 70s Tuesday and Wednesday that will be followed by highs\nin the 60s as early as Thursday.",
